\paragraph{A memory test for the candidate limiting equation.} In an independent-flight toy process let $\Psi(a)$ be survival probability for a free path longer than age $a$, with density $-\Psi'(a)$ and hazard $h(a)=-\Psi'(a)/\Psi(a)$. If $F(x,v,a,t)$ resolves time since the last collision, free transport with that collision law gives
\[
 \partial_tF+v\cdot\nabla_xF+\partial_aF=-h(a)F.
\]
Integrating over $a\geq0$ yields for $f(x,v,t)=\int F\,da$ the loss $\int h(a)F\,da$ and a gain boundary term $F(x,v,0,t)$. The marginal closes with a constant collision rate only when the hazard is constant, or when additional special structure controls the age law.

\paragraph{A concrete nonexponential example.} For $\Psi(a)=(1+a)^{-2}$, the hazard is $2/(1+a)$. Two age distributions of the same total mass, one concentrated near $a=0$ and one near a large $A$, have different losses approaching $2f$ and $2f/(1+A)$. Thus a collision operator depending only on the current $f$ cannot reproduce both. Smooth narrow age densities give the same distinction without delta functions.

\paragraph{Quasicrystal gap.} This does not assert that the source's cut-and-project scatterers have this survival law or independent flights. It explains why importing the ordinary linear Boltzmann equation before proving flight statistics is unjustified. The first required step is convergence of collision-path data for the actual quasicrystal configuration, including correlations and a suitable extended state space. Neither convergence of $f^\epsilon$ nor its final equation follows from the toy renewal calculation.

